  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=3?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{x-3}, & x<3 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{x-3}, & x>3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- We have that $f(3)$ is not defined since $x=3$ does not belong to the domain of $f(x).$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$ because $f(3)$ does not exist.",
